Version: 1.1
desc: Console Yourself (Nes_Snd_Emu, MAME, ymfm, snes_spc port) by chmaha
tags: instrument
author: chmaha
changelog:
  Layer can play a single channel: Pulse 2, Triangle or Noise on the NES, Pulse 2, Wave or Noise on the Game Boy, Tone 2, Tone 3 or Noise on the Master System.
  Game Boy and PC Engine: draw your own wave (Custom shape, saved with the project). Right-click the wave view for the preset shapes.
about:
  Chip-synth instrument with the sound hardware of classic game consoles.
  NES (2A03): two pulse channels, triangle, noise and DPCM drums, emulated with
  Nes_Snd_Emu by Shay Green, http://slack.net/~ant/libs/audio.html (LGPL-2.1+).
  Famicom expansion audio: Konami VRC6 (two pulse channels with eight duties and a sawtooth), emulated with
  Nes_Snd_Emu's Nes_Vrc6_Apu, or Konami VRC7 (six two-operator FM channels, an OPLL core with 15 built-in
  patches and one custom patch), emulated with ymfm's OPLL core by Aaron Giles (BSD-3-Clause) playing the
  VRC7 patch ROM read from the die by Nuke.YKT (as in emu2413 and the nesdev wiki).
  Game Boy (DMG): two pulse channels, wave channel and noise, emulated with MAME's
  Game Boy sound core by Wilbert Pol and Anthony Kruize, https://github.com/mamedev/mame (BSD-3-Clause).
  Master System / Game Gear (Sega PSG, SN76489): three tone channels and noise, emulated with MAME's
  SN76496 core by Nicola Salmoria and others (BSD-3-Clause).
  Mega Drive / Genesis (YM2612 or YM3438 FM plus the Sega PSG): six four-operator FM channels with
  an FM patch editor and sampled drums through the DAC, emulated with ymfm by Aaron Giles, https://github.com/aaronsgiles/ymfm (BSD-3-Clause).
  PC Engine / TurboGrafx-16 (HuC6280): six 32-step wavetable channels, noise and sampled DDA drums, emulated with MAME's
  c6280 core by Charles MacDonald (BSD-3-Clause).
  Super NES (S-DSP): eight BRR sample voices with Gaussian interpolation, hardware ADSR and the
  8-tap FIR echo, emulated with snes_spc by Shay Green, http://slack.net/~ant/libs/audio.html (LGPL-2.1+),
  playing Doug Fraker's collection of 203 BRR samples (https://github.com/nesdoug/SNES_13, public domain per its author, repository MIT),
  with four drum kits; each MIDI channel picks its own sound. Unlike real hardware, all samples stay available at once.
  Layer mode plays every note on the same set of channels; the Chips setting stacks copies of the
  chip so chords can be played, and MIDI channel 10 plays a drum kit.
  Layer can also play one NES, Game Boy or Master System channel on its own (the triangle alone for bass,
  say), and the Game Boy and PC Engine have a custom wave you draw with the mouse, saved with the project.
  Tracker mode maps MIDI channels to the hardware channels of one chip, as game music drivers did.
  JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license
  Claude generated some of this code
provides:
  Dependencies/ConsoleYourself/ConsoleYourself.txt
  Dependencies/ConsoleYourself/ConsoleYourself.flac
  Dependencies/ConsoleYourself/ConsoleYourself-LICENSE.txt

